Day 11 – Bubble Sort

Today I continued working on Bubble Sort.

Today was actually a really satisfying day because I figured out an important part of the algorithm on my own. I was trying to understand how the inner loop should decrease after every pass.

I noticed that:

First pass → 3 comparisons
Second pass → 2 comparisons
Third pass → 1 comparison

From that pattern, I came up with:

`n - i`

and used it for the inner loop:

`range(n - i)`

I didn't just copy this from somewhere. I tried different things in my workbook and figured out the pattern myself, and it worked. That made me really happy because I feel like I'm starting to think about the algorithm instead of just remembering the code.

I also understood that:

* The outer loop is used for the passes.
* The inner loop is used to compare neighboring elements.
* `j` is used to move through the neighboring elements.
* After every pass, the largest unsorted element goes to the end.
* The number of comparisons decreases after every pass.

My Code

```python
arr = [3,2,1,0]
n= len(arr)-1

for i in range(len(arr)-1):
    for j in range(n-i):
        if arr[j]>arr[j+1]:
            temp=arr[i]
            arr[j]=arr[j+1]
            arr[j+1] = temp

print(arr)
```

Time Complexity

O(n²)

I understood that Bubble Sort has an outer loop and an inner loop, so the work grows roughly like n × n.

